WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.690
Here's a train network, drawn
exactly as it lies under the city.

2
00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:06.370
The lines twist.

3
00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:10.070
Stations crowd the middle and
spread apart at the edges.

4
00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:11.110
Now watch.

5
00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.930
The curves straighten,
the gaps are evened out,

6
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.310
and the centre is drawn bigger.

7
00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:20.430
This is the style of Harry
Beck's 1933 Tube map.

8
00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:22.790
Real distances: gone.

9
00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:27.110
So why does a map that ignores
real distances still work?

10
00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:30.970
In this mission, you'll split a
problem into smaller sub-problems,

11
00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:33.990
like planning a journey:
find the nearest station,

12
00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.510
choose the route, work out the time.

13
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.770
And you'll see why a designer
might leave detail out on purpose.

14
00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:45.680
Your mission: explain how a map with
the wrong distances still gets you home.
